WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.063
Who runs security operations?

2
00:00:02.063 --> 00:00:04.137
Most agencies can name their SOC.

3
00:00:04.137 --> 00:00:08.748
Fewer can say who decided that a log
source is collected, how long it's kept,

4
00:00:08.748 --> 00:00:12.437
or who accepted the risk when
it went quiet for three weeks.

5
00:00:12.437 --> 00:00:13.926
The program assigns them.

6
00:00:13.926 --> 00:00:18.662
The CIO sets scope and funding
and designates the top-level SOC.

7
00:00:18.662 --> 00:00:23.472
The CISO decides what each plane must
emit, owns the Agency Logging Plan,

8
00:00:23.472 --> 00:00:26.838
and approves any playbook
that acts on production.

9
00:00:26.838 --> 00:00:29.512
Platform owners keep
their own planes emitting.

10
00:00:29.512 --> 00:00:32.691
Security engineering builds
the SIEM, the rules,

11
00:00:32.691 --> 00:00:35.462
the playbooks and the evidence pipeline.

12
00:00:35.462 --> 00:00:39.787
The SOC runs the queue, and CISA's
one-hour clock starts there.

13
00:00:39.787 --> 00:00:41.701
Two roles are easy to forget.

14
00:00:41.701 --> 00:00:46.041
The records officer decides disposition,
and must concur before anyone

15
00:00:46.041 --> 00:00:49.875
shortens retention, because evidence
that's gone can't come back.

16
00:00:49.875 --> 00:00:54.387
And the authorizing official, the
only official who can accept risk,

17
00:00:54.387 --> 00:00:58.577
accepts the risk of not seeing:
a gap carried rather than closed,

18
00:00:58.577 --> 00:01:01.850
a suppression with no end
date, a missed deadline.
